-### Parameters
-*   **$\theta$:** Shape parameter. Derivatives evaluated at $\theta=0$ [S2].
-*   **Reference Configuration $\mathcal{B}$:** Typically $\mathcal{M}(\theta_0)$ [S3, unofficial].
-
 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Visibility Discontinuities:** If Assumption S.1 is violated (refractive/reflective boundaries or $\theta$-dependent shadows), the velocity of visibility boundaries is nontrivial to compute without tetrahedralization [S2].
-*   **Multiple Boundary Vertices:** Paths with two or more vertices on the boundary have zero measure and contribute nothing to the first-order derivative [S2].
-*   **Computational Cost:** Gradient computation for a single image takes seconds to tens of seconds, making it impractical for neural network training [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Tetrahedralization:** Previous methods required tetrahedralization to interpolate mapping $X$ into the volume interior. This method avoids that overhead [S2].
-
-### Subtopics
-#### Unidirectional Estimator
-Samples paths from camera to light. Cited as a Monte Carlo estimator for the boundary path integral [S2].
-
-#### Bidirectional Estimator
-Samples paths from camera and light, connecting them. Cited as a Monte Carlo estimator for the boundary path integral [S2].
-
-#### Multiple Importance Sampling (MIS)
-Combines unidirectional and bidirectional estimates to reduce variance. Cited as a Monte Carlo estimator for the boundary path integral [S2].
+*   **Visibility Discontinuities:** The derivation assumes no refractive/reflective boundaries or $\theta$-dependent shadows (Assumption S.1) [S2]. If violated, the velocity of visibility boundaries is nontrivial to compute without tetrahedralization [S2].
+*   **Multiple Boundary Vertices:** Paths with two or more vertices constrained on the boundary have zero measure and make no contribution to the first-order derivatives [S2].
+*   **Performance:** Computation of gradients for a single rendered image takes between a few seconds to tens of seconds, making it impractical for training neural networks [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Tetrahedralization Avoidance:** Unlike previous methods (e.g., Zhang et al. [ZYZ21]), this method avoids tetrahedralizations and point-in-tetrahedron lookups by moving terms to the boundary [S2].
